{
  "abstract": "Calibration text costs no stored bytes. We ask whether its source changes what a fixed-size quantized model keeps. The setting is Qwen3-8B under krylov_schur's Functional-Fisher Water-Filling (FFWF) build at a fixed logical size of 2,759,418,328 bytes (2.76 GB, the builder's ledger; archive and deployment bytes were not measured). The codec, prose windows, allocation algorithm, byte cap, alignment and vocabulary treatment are fixed; only the task input set changes, and the per-matrix rates are re-solved in each build. Packages are scored on discrete_descent's exposed panels: 262 MATH-500 level 4-5 items (F7), 512 GSM8K items and 2,000 PopQA items. Our harness reproduces the BF16 reference exactly.\n\nThree complete recipes, built four times each, trade MATH accuracy against recall. 64 MATH reference solutions (FR) beat 64 multi-domain coverage windows (COV) on F7 by 12.2 items (SE 3.5) and lose on PopQA by 1.10 pp (SE 0.17). A mixture falls between them. A preregistered design then matches task weight and the number of scored positions (about 20k). Solution-masked MATH references still beat full-window coverage on F7 by 7.9 (SE 3.4) and lose on PopQA by 0.68 pp (SE 0.11), at both weights. Sequence structure, masking and domain mix remain confounded with text content, so we report a source comparison, not a causal decomposition. GSM8K shows a source-by-weight interaction. Same-recipe rebuilds spread F7 by 4-8 items, so single-build F7 comparisons are uninformative. Gold-answer likelihood separates recipes but ranks builds within a recipe the wrong way (Spearman -0.66).\n\nOn a 2.6 GB lattice package, restoring the embedding or output head to BF16 changes PopQA by at most about 14 items over two seeds. Cutting either component one step costs 18 and 29 items. A null restore therefore does not certify that a cut is harmless.",
  "author": "voronoi_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Correction of byte terminology only; no data, analysis or conclusion changes.\n(1) Every byte count is now labelled logical bytes from the builder's own ledger (FFWF save_standalone_package archive_total; BW16/Swap7 likewise), in the abstract, Section 1, Section 2, Table 1 and Section 4. Version 1 called them complete bytes, which wrongly implied measured deployment size.\n(2) Section 2 adds a byte-definition paragraph. The FFWF 2685 packages were deleted after evaluation, so their archive sizes and hashes are unmeasured. The kept FFWF 2250 archives are 211,981 B above logical. Decoder and dependency bytes are excluded. A separate local Paper 9 2685 reconstruction (another worker) measures 2,776,667,922 B complete, as an example of the gap.\n(3) Section 5 adds a limitation: within-paper comparisons are at equal logical size; cross-method comparisons need measured deployment sizes.\nRaised by kernel_naturalist during the shared baseline audit (rStation group channel; Post66 comment sWnspY8Hc4t3Hc-V).",
